title: Psalm 50: God Is Judge
description: Psalm 50 read as a courtroom in four movements: the summons, the sacrifice God seeks, the indictment, and the verdict and promise.
output: lessons/bible/psalm-50.html

# Rebuilt on the deck template from lessons/bible/psalm-50.html, slide for slide. The two decorative SVGs are dropped.

=== Introduction
--- God Is Judge Himself [title]
overline: A Psalm of Asaph
sub: Psalm 50 and the case the mighty God brings against His own people
big: Psalm 50:1
hint: Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- Psalm 50 in four movements
kicker: The Text
lead: The whole psalm is a courtroom. God summons the court, sets aside what He is *not* charging, brings the indictment, and then gives the verdict and the promise.
xr: Psalm 50:1-6 | **The Summons** (vv. 1–6). The Judge comes out of Zion and gathers His saints
xr: Psalm 50:7-15 | **The Sacrifice God Seeks** (vv. 7–15). Not bulls and goats, but thanksgiving and calling on Him
xr: Psalm 50:16-21 | **The Indictment** (vv. 16–21). The charges against those who take His covenant in their mouth
xr: Psalm 50:22-23 | **Verdict and Promise** (vv. 22–23). A warning to those that forget God, and salvation shewn to the faithful
center: [[Psalm 50:1-23|Read the whole psalm]]

--- The lesson
kicker: Contents
tile: I | The Author and His Seer
tile: II | Our God Shall Come
tile: III | Not Thy Sacrifices
tile: IV | The Offering God Seeks
tile: V | The Indictment
tile: VI | The Case Against Us
tile: VII | Consider This

=== I. The Author and His Seer
--- God speaks in the first person
kicker: I · The Author and His Seer
scripture: Psalm 50:1
lead: Psalm 50 is a song written in the voice of God Himself, His own perspective spoken directly to man. It is instruction in doctrine and in prophecy, given by His word, in the first person.
p: The psalm opens by introducing its Author: *the mighty God*. All scripture is given by inspiration of God ([[2 Timothy 3:16]]). It is God-breathed and God-spoken. But this psalm is set apart as the word of God delivered as a message of judgment for His people.

--- Asaph, the seer
kicker: I · The Author and His Seer
lead: God spoke these words through a psalmist other than David. The superscription titles it “A Psalm of Asaph,” the first of the twelve Asaphite psalms in the Bible.
p: Asaph was a prominent Levite, appointed by King David as the chief musician and worship leader before the Ark of the Covenant when it was brought to Jerusalem ([[1 Chronicles 16:4-5]]). He was more than a song director. Scripture credits him as a prophet, and calls him a *seer*.
xr: 2 Chronicles 29:30 | “…sing praise unto the {Lord} with the words of David, and of Asaph the seer.”
panel: Prophesying | May refer to preaching and declaring the word of God.
panel: Seeing | The specific term for a visionary given glimpses of the future by God: prophetic understanding of things that are not, but will be.

--- The Word who called the earth
kicker: I · The Author and His Seer
lead: The psalm first establishes the primacy of God the Creator. By His Word the earth and the heavens were made.
xr: John 1:1-4 | “All things were made by him; and without him was not any thing made that was made.”
xr: 1 Corinthians 8:6 | “…and one Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by him.”
p: When the Bible credits creation to God, remember that this includes Jesus. We read the Old Testament revelation with a New Testament understanding. The nature of the Godhead is revealed in the creation, and it matters for this psalm: the God who called the earth is the God who will come to judge it.

=== II. Our God Shall Come
--- Out of Zion God hath shined
kicker: II · Our God Shall Come
scripture: Psalm 50:2
lead: Zion is the place God chose to put His name.
p: His presence in the Tabernacle and later the Temple, God dwelling with His people through their worship, is an echo of the true coming of the Lord in the flesh, and of His return on His day to judge the nations upon that holy mountain.
panel: Tabernacle | God among His people in worship
panel: Incarnation | God come in the flesh
panel: Return | God come to judge on His holy mountain

--- A fire shall devour before Him
kicker: II · Our God Shall Come
scripture: Psalm 50:3
p: The Second Coming, the return of Christ, is the day we look forward to with gladness. He will come with power, a consuming fire proceeding from Him, and the sword of His word from His mouth shall slay the wicked.
xr: 2 Thessalonians 1:7-9 | “…in flaming fire taking vengeance on them that know not God…”
xr: Revelation 19:15 | “And out of his mouth goeth a sharp sword…”
p: The future judgment of the world was a key part of Israel’s understanding, though they saw it through a glass, darkly ([[1 Corinthians 13:12]]). With the full revelation of the completed Bible, we see clearly what they only glimpsed in faith.

--- Gather my saints together
kicker: II · Our God Shall Come
scripture: Psalm 50:4-5
p: He comes as Judge, the Son calling up to heaven and down to earth, with a shout, with the trump: the call to judgment of His people, before His wrath is poured out on the world.
xr: 1 Thessalonians 4:16-17 | “…caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air…”
lead: The gathering of the elect, those sanctified by the Spirit in Christ, is not a “new doctrine,” as some would claim. The Rapture was a key part of prophecy for the Lord’s people, sung by Asaph the seer.

--- A covenant made by sacrifice
kicker: II · Our God Shall Come
scripture: Psalm 50:6
p: The covenant Moses made between the people and God was sealed with blood that represented the blood of Christ, and with it the promise of a better covenant made by a better sacrifice: the sacrifice of the Son.
xr: Hebrews 9:19-23 | “…without shedding of blood is no remission.”
p: The heavens declare the righteousness of Christ Jesus, and His righteousness is upon all His people.
panel: The Judge | Himself
panel: The Atonement | Himself
panel: The Mediator | Himself
take: The saints are secure in His righteousness. But now God speaks to Israel in judgment, beginning with His own people.

=== III. Not Thy Sacrifices
--- Hear, O my people
kicker: III · Not Thy Sacrifices
scripture: Psalm 50:7-8
lead: The psalm turns from the future prophecy to the present call.
p: The sacrifices had been kept. Israel maintained the Levitical ordinances on which God had based His promises, the constant offerings that preserved ritual cleanliness and kept His presence among them. Those offerings stayed His hand from reproving them harshly. He is not asking for more sacrifice. No additional bulls or goats need shed their blood.

--- The better sacrifice
kicker: III · Not Thy Sacrifices
lead: Under the old covenant the blood of bulls and goats was offered continually by the Levites, each one signifying the Sacrifice to come. We have that better sacrifice, once and for all: a covenant made by the blood of Jesus, the burnt offering for our sin.
xr: Hebrews 9:11-14 | “How much more shall the blood of Christ… purge your conscience from dead works to serve the living God?”

--- The cattle upon a thousand hills
kicker: III · Not Thy Sacrifices
scripture: Psalm 50:9-13
p: Every beast is His. If He were hungry, wanting the savour of the fat and the sustenance of meat, He would have His pick and His fill. He does not need them.
lead: Israel’s sacrifices were a picture, and a necessity of the covenant, but they did not please Him in and of themselves.

--- Mercy, and not sacrifice
kicker: III · Not Thy Sacrifices
fig: The prophet | Hosea 6:6
fig: The Lord Jesus | Matthew 12:7
lead: The same sentiment, spoken by Hosea and repeated by Christ. What God was after in Psalm 50 is what He is still after.

=== IV. The Offering God Seeks
--- Offer unto God thanksgiving
kicker: IV · The Offering God Seeks
scripture: Psalm 50:14
lead: God makes it plain that the offering is thanksgiving.
p: Their vows were to be paid to keep their part in the covenant as His nation: tithes and firstfruits as acts of faith. Unlike the offerings to other gods, it was not a meal for Him to consume. It was thanksgiving, offered in gratitude for all He had supplied, and to keep themselves unspotted as His people.
p: God desires mercy toward others: right judgment, defending the weak, and hating the workers of iniquity.
xr: Matthew 23:23 | “…and have omitted the weightier matters of the law, judgment, mercy, and faith…”
take: These are not the repeated acts of priests, but matters of the heart, which God judges and which He seeks from us.

--- Call upon me in the day of trouble
kicker: IV · The Offering God Seeks
scripture: Psalm 50:15
lead: Having established His ways, God gives the gospel: good news to all men in all ages, the dispensation of grace.
p: Calling upon the name of the Lord for salvation, that we may glorify Him before all men. It is the promise of salvation and the heart of the Great Commission, joined to the truth that when we are in Him we magnify His light, so that men see our works of righteousness, mercy and thanksgiving, and glorify God.
xr: Matthew 5:16 | “Let your light so shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father which is in heaven.”
take: This is our purpose as believers: to make known the judgment of God and to be an example of His mercy, praising His name in thanksgiving, all openly before men, that He may be glorified.

=== V. The Indictment
--- The charge of formalism
kicker: V · The Indictment
scripture: Psalm 50:16-17
p: Having summoned the court and stated His position, God begins the indictment. Having shown that He has no need of bulls, goats or fowls, He lays the charge of *formalism* against His covenant people.
lead: The religious practices are being kept, the bare minimum of service to maintain the covenant. The Lord is not accusing them of failing there, but in the weightier matters, just as Jesus accused the Pharisees.

--- A light to all nations
kicker: V · The Indictment
lead: God gave Israel promises and practices to keep them as His people, but they were failing to declare His statutes and proclaim His law.
p: The whole point of Israel was to be a light to all men, not to selfishly reap the benefits while doing nothing to teach the nations by example. They squandered their blessings by not fulfilling that role.
xr: Deuteronomy 4:5-6 | “…Surely this great nation is a wise and understanding people.”

--- Near with their lips, far in their heart
kicker: V · The Indictment
lead: God accuses the wicked among His people of missing the entire point, the same charge Jesus would bring against the religious leaders of the Jews.
xr: Matthew 15:8 | “This people draweth nigh unto me with their mouth… but their heart is far from me.”
xr: Matthew 23:27-28 | “…ye are like unto whited sepulchres, which indeed appear beautiful outward…”

--- The counts against them
kicker: V · The Indictment
p: God’s accusations are sharp. ([[Psalm 50:18-20|vv. 18–20]])
panel: Consent | *“When thou sawest a thief, then thou consentedst with him.”* They went along with the thief.
panel: Partaking | *“…and hast been partaker with adulterers.”* Both the literal engagement with the world, and the spiritual adultery of general ecumenicalism.
panel: Deceit | *“Thou givest thy mouth to evil, and thy tongue frameth deceit.”* They speak evil things, and lie.
panel: Slander | *“Thou sittest and speakest against thy brother.”* They not only fail to love their neighbour, they lie against their own brother.

--- “And I kept silence”
kicker: V · The Indictment
scripture: Psalm 50:21
lead: God is longsuffering and merciful, not willing that any should perish ([[2 Peter 3:9]]). He gave them space and time to reconsider their ways.
p: Instead they considered themselves holy as God is holy. They thought that by their religious practice, keeping the letter of the law, they could ignore the purpose and spirit of the law. It is the same indictment as Matthew 23, and it could be said of so many supposed Christians today.

=== VI. The Case Against Us
--- The leaven of the Pharisees
kicker: VI · The Case Against Us
lead: For Christians today, the indictment is the same leaven of the Pharisees: hypocrisy. ([[Luke 12:1]])
p: Just as the Pharisees had the Old Testament sacrifices, we have the once-and-for-all sacrifice of Christ and the total remission of sins.
xr: Hebrews 10:8 | “Sacrifice and offering and burnt offerings and offering for sin thou wouldest not, neither hadst pleasure therein…”

--- No more sacrifice
kicker: VI · The Case Against Us
p: As God told Israel He was not seeking to add sacrifices to those they already offered, He is not asking us for additional acts of atonement. He remembers our sins no more.
fig: For those in Christ | Hebrews 10:17-18
fig: For wilful sin | Hebrews 10:26-27
lead: Like Israel in Psalm 50, we are covered by the blood. We are His people. But are we bound by formalism and the outward appearance of being clean, while failing at the good work He saved us for, the work that gives Him glory?

--- Taking His covenant in our mouth
kicker: VI · The Case Against Us
lead: If we are not declaring His righteousness and glory in thanksgiving, and showing mercy and judgment to others, loving our neighbour, how are we taking His covenant in our mouths?
p: How are we telling the world we are Christians when we are not acting like it? Who does it benefit?
xr: James 2:14-17 | “Even so faith, if it hath not works, is dead, being alone.”

--- This is not legalism
kicker: VI · The Case Against Us
lead: God is not saying that we must do works to be saved, or to be His. We are not justified before God by our works, but before men. Yet God does have works for us to do.
panel: Before God | Justified by faith, through the blood of Christ alone.
panel: Before men | Shown to be His by the good works He ordained for us.
xr: Ephesians 2:10 | “For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works…”

--- The buried talent
kicker: VI · The Case Against Us
p: The indictment against His people is that while we have availed ourselves of His covenant and promises, we have squandered the talents of silver He has given us. ([[Matthew 25:14-30]])
panel: Five talents | Traded, and gained five more. *“Well done, thou good and faithful servant.”*
panel: Two talents | Gained two more. *“Well done, good and faithful servant.”*
panel: One talent | Afraid, went and digged in the earth, and hid it. *“Thou wicked and slothful servant.”*
lead: To whom much is given, much is required ([[Luke 12:48]]). There are professing Christians who have taken the offer and buried it. They never took it in as their own, nurtured it, or used it to increase God’s kingdom.
take: Again, this is not works-based salvation. Had the servant kept his one talent in his own care, he would not have been cast out. Instead he buried it and went through the motions.

--- Not forsaking the assembling
kicker: VI · The Case Against Us
p: Hebrews 10 sets the command to gather between its two statements on sacrifice: no more need of sacrifice for those in Christ, and no more sacrifice for wilful sin.
xr: Hebrews 10:23-25 | “Not forsaking the assembling of ourselves together… but exhorting one another…”
lead: We are commanded not to forsake the assembly, and its purpose is to love one another: edifying, building each other up, so much the more as the day approaches.
p: Yet some treat church as a checkbox for their righteousness, proving themselves holy by attendance alone. They wash the outside of the cup ([[Matthew 23:25-27]]) while within they are whited tombs full of dead men’s bones. To them religion is rituals and obligations that benefit only themselves, sacraments as their sacrifice rather than the blood of Christ.

=== VII. Consider This
--- Ye that forget God
kicker: VII · Consider This
scripture: Psalm 50:22
lead: Christians in name only go through the motions and never once consider God.
p: God goes out of His way in this psalm to stipulate that His righteous people are protected under His care. His case is with the false professors of His name, who think the formalism of their worship is enough to spare them from His wrath. They forget God, and He will tear them in pieces. They bury their talent out of fear, while never truly fearing God.

--- Whoso offereth praise
kicker: VII · Consider This
scripture: Psalm 50:23
lead: But God, ever gracious, ends on a note of promise.
p: Our purpose as His people is to praise His name, that He be glorified among men. We are the salt of the earth, preserving the world against judgment, and He encourages us to continue in praise and thanksgiving.
xr: Matthew 5:13 | “…but if the salt have lost his savour, wherewith shall it be salted?”
xr: Revelation 3:16 | “So then because thou art lukewarm… I will spue thee out of my mouth.”
xr: Revelation 2:5 | “…or else I will come unto thee quickly, and will remove thy candlestick…”
take: Salt can lose its savour. A lukewarm church can have its candlestick removed, and be of no further use to God.

--- Stay salty
kicker: VII · Consider This
lead: God’s case against Israel is God’s case against all who call themselves by His name and fail to live to proclaim His glory. Let us then keep our savour:
- Do all things with mercy and judgment
- Forgive those who trespass against us
- Consider the poor, the widow and the fatherless
- Exhort one another, so much the more as the day approaches
- Offer praise and thanksgiving, that God be glorified among men
p: So that at the coming of Christ, when He brings fiery judgment upon the world, we will be found acceptable: *“Well done, thou good and faithful servant.”* ([[Matthew 25:21]])
take: Amen
